# TykoTech-MCP — the slim image shape (SPECS/ROADMAP.md R11, D-009 reopen).
#
# Same product, less of it *to attack*. The bundled image ships the Deno
# toolchain, the module cache and the whole source tree, because that is what
# `deno run` needs. This one ships a single compiled binary (R15) and nothing
# else of ours: no interpreter to invoke, no lockfile, no `packages/`, no tests.
#
# What it does NOT buy is size, and pretending otherwise would be dishonest.
# Re-measured on 2026-09-11 at 0.2.0: 409 MB against the bundled image's 425 MB
# — about 3.8%. The compiled binary is ~100 MB and largely replaces what it
# removes, while Chromium and ffmpeg account for most of both images. Choose
# this shape because you want no interpreter and no source in the runtime, not
# because you want a smaller download.
#
# Those are `docker image inspect --format '{{.Size}}'` figures: the image's own
# uncompressed layer total. `docker images` reports ~1.58 GB and ~1.63 GB on a
# containerd-backed engine, because that column measures the unpacked snapshot
# on disk instead. Both are real; they answer different questions, and the older
# revision of this comment did not say which one it meant.
#
# ## The permission matrix: the same egress, wider file and env grants (D-053, D-151)
#
# The bundled image's grants live in its `CMD`, so an operator can scope or
# widen them per deployment. **A compiled binary has no CMD to edit.**
# `scripts/compile.ts` bakes the matrix at build time and it cannot be
# re-flagged at launch, so it passes a bare `--allow-net` — a binary cannot know
# which address a deployment will bind — and bare `--allow-read`,
# `--allow-write` and `--allow-env`.
#
# Until D-151 that made the two shapes differ in what they could dial: the
# bundled `CMD` granted the bind and loopback only, so `browser_head` (whose
# availability predicate is a `Deno.permissions.query` on a public host) was
# offered here and withheld there, and a model provider was `reachable: true`
# here and `false` there. D-151 (owner-approved, 2026-10-07) gave the bundled
# `CMD` the same unscoped net grant, so both shapes now offer `browser_head`,
# reach the providers the file names (`builtinAgent: true` for the same
# fixture) and leave the server's egress to the code that dials:
# `browser_head`'s literal-address deny and `TTK_ALLOWED_DOMAINS` (unrestricted
# by default), and the providers file as the only allowlist for model hosts.
# The difference left is the file and env grants, and that the bundled image
# can still be scoped back with a compose `command:` override while this one
# cannot.
#
# `tests/container/verify_container.ts` asserts the egress-dependent tools
# alike for both shapes, so neither image can drift away from the other
# unnoticed.
#
# It is *slim*, not distroless, and the difference is deliberate rather than a
# compromise. Chromium is a dynamically linked program with a long tail of
# dependencies — fontconfig, NSS, dbus, a dozen X and GTK libraries — and the
# setuid sandbox helper it must use is a Debian package with its own ownership
# and permission bits. Assembling that by hand on a distroless base would mean
# maintaining an undocumented copy of Debian's own dependency graph, and the
# first thing to break would be the sandbox. A trimmed Debian keeps the package
# manager's answer to that question and drops everything else.
#
# The security posture is identical to the bundled image: `--no-sandbox` is
# refused in TTK_BROWSER_ARGS and TTK_CHROMIUM_NO_SANDBOX is never set (D-094),
# the setuid helper is present, and the container still needs
# `no-new-privileges` dropped plus the custom seccomp profile in docker/.
# See SPECS/SECURITY-AUTH.md §6.

# Base images pinned by digest, tags kept for the reader (C13, D-103; see the
# bundled Dockerfile for how to move them). The Deno digest is the bundled
# image's: denoland/deno:2.9.7, resolved 2026-10-04.
ARG DENO_IMAGE=denoland/deno:2.9.7@sha256:fa335acdf6b72106eda2cb6a8cb5f4187e7630e357467489db4b2e7352d5e432
# debian:stable-slim, resolved 2026-10-04.
ARG RUNTIME_IMAGE=debian:stable-slim@sha256:5bc3287b25407c965a30f38e32603dc253a3869e1b12a21ac09bfc27fd8b13ce

# --- build ------------------------------------------------------------------
FROM ${DENO_IMAGE} AS build
WORKDIR /app

COPY deno.json deno.lock ./
COPY packages ./packages
COPY scripts ./scripts
COPY tests ./tests

RUN deno cache --frozen packages/cli/mod.ts scripts/healthcheck.ts
# The SPA is compiled *into* the binary, so it is built first.
RUN deno task build:web
# The permission matrix is baked in here and cannot be widened at launch; the
# Chromium path is passed because a compiled grant has to name a real file.
RUN deno run -A scripts/compile.ts --out /out --chromium /usr/bin/chromium

# --- runtime ----------------------------------------------------------------
FROM ${RUNTIME_IMAGE}

# Chromium, its sandbox helper, fonts, and the encoder R7 shells out to.
# `--no-install-recommends` is what makes this shape worth having.
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
	chromium \
	chromium-sandbox \
	fonts-liberation \
	ca-certificates \
	dumb-init \
	ffmpeg \
	&& rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
	&& apt-get clean

# An unprivileged user, created here because there is no base image to inherit
# one from. Its uid (1000) is NOT the bundled image's: that image runs as
# `deno`, uid 1993 (measured in the 2.9.7 base), which docker-compose.yml also
# sets — so moving a /data volume between the two shapes needs a chown. (An
# older revision of this comment claimed the two matched.)
RUN groupadd --gid 1000 ttk \
	&& useradd --uid 1000 --gid 1000 --create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin ttk

COPY --from=build --chown=ttk:ttk /out/tykotech-mcp /usr/local/bin/tykotech-mcp
# D-154: the bundled image's maintenance name (`ttk seed --force`, `ttk doctor`), so one compose command serves both
# shapes. Here it is the binary itself, with its baked matrix: a compiled binary cannot be re-flagged.
RUN ln -s /usr/local/bin/tykotech-mcp /usr/local/bin/ttk

# Chromium needs a writable HOME for its crashpad handler (E-08); without one it
# dies with SIGTRAP before the CDP endpoint comes up. /data is the volume.
RUN mkdir -p /data /home/ttk/.cache \
	&& chown -R ttk:ttk /data /home/ttk

# Configuration only — never a secret. Secrets arrive as *_FILE paths and are
# read inside the process, so they never enter an image layer or the environment
# Chromium inherits.
ENV TTK_LOG_FORMAT=json \
	TTK_BROWSER_PATH=/usr/bin/chromium \
	TTK_DATA_DIR=/data \
	TTK_FFMPEG=/usr/bin/ffmpeg \
	TTK_HEADLESS=true \
	TTK_HOST=0.0.0.0 \
	TTK_PORT=8787

# R14 (D-122): the MCP Registry's OCI ownership proof, matching `name` in server.json. Metadata only, no grant;
# the bundled Dockerfile carries the same label.
LABEL io.modelcontextprotocol.server.name="eu.tykotech/tykotech-mcp"

USER ttk
WORKDIR /home/ttk
VOLUME ["/data"]

# 8787 only. The CDP port is never published: it is an unauthenticated remote
# control for the browser.
EXPOSE 8787

# The binary answers its own health check, so this image needs no second
# runtime to run a script with.
HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s --start-period=20s --retries=3 \
	CMD ["/usr/local/bin/tykotech-mcp", "healthcheck"]

# dumb-init reaps the process tree Chromium leaves behind.
ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/bin/dumb-init", "--"]
CMD ["/usr/local/bin/tykotech-mcp", "serve"]
